The problem arises from the mutable default argument `DEFAULT_TAGS`. Python computes default arguments only once, at the moment the function is defined, so every call to `make_post` that skips the `tags` argument reuses the same list object. When tests call `make_post` without explicit tags, they all modify this shared list by appending `"post"`, causing the list to grow across test runs — the assertion passes only when this test runs first and independently.

To fix this, ensure each call gets its own list:

```python
def make_post(title, tags=None):
    if tags is None:
        tags = list(DEFAULT_TAGS)
    tags.append("post")
    return {"title": title, "tags": tags}
```

A mutable default argument `DEFAULT_TAGS` is responsible for this issue. Since Python evaluates default arguments one time at the moment the function is defined, every invocation of `make_post` that doesn't provide a `tags` parameter ends up using the same list instance. When multiple tests call `make_post` without passing explicit tags, they each add `"post"` to this identical list, causing it to expand with each test run — consequently, the assertion only passes in this particular test when it happens to run before all others or in isolation.

The solution is to ensure each function call receives its own fresh list:

```python
def make_post(title, tags=None):
    if tags is None:
        tags = list(DEFAULT_TAGS)
    tags.append("post")
    return {"title": title, "tags": tags}
```

The problem arises from the mutable default argument `DEFAULT_TAGS`. Python evaluates default arguments once at function definition time rather than at each call, so every invocation of `make_post` that omits the `tags` argument references the same list object. When tests invoke `make_post` without providing an explicit `tags` parameter, they each append `"post"` to this shared list, causing it to accumulate across test runs. The assertion therefore succeeds only when this particular test executes first or runs in isolation, before other tests have grown the list.

The solution is to create a new list for each function call. This can be done by checking whether `tags` is `None` and, if so, creating a fresh copy of `DEFAULT_TAGS`:

```python
def make_post(title, tags=None):
    if tags is None:
        tags = list(DEFAULT_TAGS)
    tags.append("post")
    return {"title": title, "tags": tags}
```

